  :adr/body
  "## The shape

**An operation with two or more effects, where a failure between them leaves
state that nothing can name or undo.** The question that decides the ordering is
not which comes first logically but **which residue the system can still see and
repair.**

Two instances were fixed in `kotoba-lang/vpn` (ADR-2608122800). This is the
sweep for the rest: 52,832 source files under `orgs/`, by token-set intersection
rather than repeated walks.

## The invariant, and the three verbs that each need a different order

*The record never stops naming a resource that exists, and no resource exists
the record never named.*

- **create** -> record first, bind second, compensating delete
  (`provisionDevice`, fixed)
- **move** -> bind new first, record, unbind old last (`rotateKey`, fixed —
  removal last because its failure is harmless)
- **destroy** -> **unbind first, delete the record last** (`revokeDevice`,
  **still wrong**)

## #1 is in the same file, fifty lines below the comment that explains it

`revokeDevice` deletes the row — a **hard `DELETE … RETURNING`, no tombstone** —
then removes the peer. Every lookup reads that table, so the residue is **a bound
tunnel nothing can name**. And the failure is **explicitly swallowed**
(`# non-fatal`), returning `{\"ok\": True}`.

**A revocation that reports success while leaving the tunnel up, on a no-logs
privacy VPN.**

The comment written to explain the `provisionDevice` fix names the exact
property that breaks this one — *not reachable by revokeDevice, which finds the
key to remove by looking the device up* — **fifty lines above the function that
deletes that row first.** No ADR mentioned `revokeDevice`.

**FIXED, merge `fd2622ca23ca`, pin advanced.** Now: read the device (ownership
decided before any mutation), unbind, delete last — with a 503 and a named log
line if the delete fails.

**The idempotency the fix rests on was traced, not assumed.** `wg set … peer
<key> remove` maps to a remove flag, and the kernel's `set_peer()` assigns
success **before** the hashtable lookup, so an absent peer takes the early exit
and returns 0. `wg` exits non-zero only when the ipc call itself fails, so
wg-agent's checked run does not fire and its handler returns ok without ever
consulting the peer list. The deployed path is that same in-tree kernel module.
**Honest limit stated by the agent: macOS has no kernel WireGuard, so the exit
status for an absent PEER is established from source, not from a live run.**

**One deliberate non-fatal remains, and it is narrow.** A 422 from the agent
means the key was refused by the same validator that guards an add — so it was
never bound, there is nothing to unbind, and treating it as fatal would make
such a row **permanently undeletable**. That row is exactly the residue
`provisionDevice` leaves when it cannot withdraw a record, **which it leaves
because this route can clear it.** Transport failure, 5xx and 403 are all fatal
now.

50 to 63 tests. Reverting only the source keeps 7 failures, with 6 of the new
tests passing both ways as controls. The simulated failures are the three that
matter — unbind refuses, exit node refuses the credential, delete refuses — and
each asserts **what is left behind and that an ordinary retry finishes it**,
not merely the status code.

## #2 is a fleet-wide chokepoint that RE-EXECUTES

`network-awai/cloud-itonami`'s approval handler performs the effect — a real
Resend send, a Stripe object, a **billed** GPU job — then transacts the status.

**`approved-effects` selects exactly `:approved`, so the next sweep re-executes
it.** No idempotency key on any path. And its `catch` marks `:failed` when the
*transact* fails after a successful send — **a delivered email recorded as never
sent.**

Related, same repo: a GPU submit whose returned job id is the **only** handle
(the poll endpoint needs exactly it) is discarded on failure, so the job cannot
be listed, polled or cancelled — and the effect stays `:approved`, so **the next
sweep bills a second one.** And a Stripe product+price pair where **the product
id is never stored even on success**, sent with **no `Idempotency-Key`**.

**The fleet already contains the fix**: one path sends `Idempotency-Key
<effect-id>` and cannot double-issue. Applying that makes mark-before / mark-
after safe, because the retry becomes free.

## #3 does not need a crash

`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app` dispatches a fax down a phone line, then
records. **`:provider-unreadable` is only reachable AFTER a 2xx** — the provider
accepted the transmission — and the failure branch **writes nothing**, so the
record still shows it un-dispatched, **inviting a re-send of a legal document.**
The discarded `:provider-id` is the only join back from the provider's callback.

**The rule is already written in that same repository**, in `credential.clj`:
*the index is allocated from durable state before signing, so a credential that
exists is always revocable. Allocating afterwards would leave a window in which
an issued credential had no index to flip.* Articulated, and not applied to fax.

## The rest, ranked

Vault: a plaintext secret PUT into a live Worker before the audit INSERT — and
the repo calls that decrypt a *bounded* zero-knowledge violation, where **the
audit row IS the bound.** Two YouTube publishers that upload before persisting
the id, one defaulting to **public**, neither with an already-uploaded guard, so
**the natural retry duplicates**. An agent DID minted with its signing key before
the graph vertices are written, the whole block wrapped in
`catch -> console.warn`, returning 200 with the field silently omitted. A public
repo created before any manifest entry — **its own header records this having
happened once.** A newscaster whose upload returns `nil` on non-2xx, so an upload
YouTube accepted logs `:publish-failed` — **an actively wrong record.**

**Both YouTube paths already obtain the resumable-upload session URL before any
bytes are committed, and discard it.** Persisting that one string converts an
unnameable orphan into a resumable job.

**FIXED — three merges, pins advanced.** All three had checkout == west pin ==
`origin/main`, so no three-way drift for once.

**The newscaster case was not an ordering fix**, and establishing that mattered.
`upload!` collapsed **four** outcomes into `nil` — file missing, the service
refusing, transport dying mid-flight, and a 2xx with no id. Once the response is
kept the record follows correctly. Only a 4xx is now a determinate rejection;
transport and 5xx and no-id are **held as indeterminate, never recorded as
failed.** The ordering half was separate and real: nothing recorded intent, so an
attempt marker now precedes the effect.

**The tests caught the agent's own first fix having the same defect one level
down.** It released the upload session as soon as the PUT returned — **while the
durable record still lacked the id.** The session now carries the id in an
`uploaded` state and is released only after persist. That is the shape repeating
inside its own repair, and only a retry test surfaced it.

**And a weak assertion nearly passed against the broken implementation**:
`.indexOf` returns `-1` for an effect that was never called, so an
ordering check comparing positions succeeded when neither call happened.
Tightened to assert the call occurred **before** asserting where it occurred.
**An ordering test that does not first assert existence is not an ordering
test.**

Coverage was not uniform and is stated per repo: one had a real suite, one had
23 tests, and **one had no test setup at all** — no runner, no config, no
dependency. Its before/after is against a faithful transcription rather than the
literal old file, because the logic lived in a self-executing script that cannot
be required; it was extracted to make it testable.

**One decision left open deliberately.** `public` remains the default in the
syndication publisher. It is defensible under that repo's stated model — the
work is already public — and **changing a publish default silently changes what
operators get, which is a product call rather than an ordering defect.** The
ordering fix removes the compounding half, since the retry no longer duplicates,
and the effective value is now printed before the upload rather than only in a
dry run.

## What the sweep refuted, which matters as much

A flagged **unconditional AWS credential leak** — an access-key pair returned on
every call and persisted nowhere. Traced: the only occurrences of that endpoint
or of signature *verification* anywhere are inside the file itself, its own doc
comment and the response field. **No verifier, no route**, and the endpoint
belongs to a retired product. **Not an unrevocable credential — a fabricated one
nothing can accept.** A trust bug in the response, not this pattern.

Also correct by construction, reported so nobody re-investigates: a payment
settler with all writes in one batch and idempotent on its key; a key rotation
with history and main in one batch; an API-key path delegated to one store with
**no local mirror**, hence always listable and revocable — **200 lines below the
agent-session path whose mirror creates the gap.**

## Two silent-zero search failures, caught only by a control

`rg --files-from` is not a real flag, and an `xargs -a <(…)` form also yielded
nothing. **Both intersections looked empty and clean.** They were caught by
running a known-positive control.

**A count of zero is not evidence until the search is proven able to find
something.** That is the third instance of this class today, after a regex that
returned 2.3M garbage hits and a scan that crashed on a 512 MB output.

## Scope limit, stated rather than buried

**Everything below #4 was read from the checkout, not the west pin**, and those
pin objects are not fetched locally — eight repos have checkout != pin.
Confirming at the pinned tree needs a fetch. Findings #1-3 and #8 are at trees
where checkout equals pin.

Also undetermined: **whether these paths are live.** The vpn ADR states Phase 0
with no users; no equivalent was established for the Resend/Stripe/GPU handlers,
**and liveness is what separates #2 from a latent defect.**

And structural: **no script reconciles an org's GitHub repos against
`west.yml`** — the two orphan audits are both local-filesystem-first, so a repo
existing on GitHub with no local directory is invisible to both. That is why the
public-repo residue stayed hidden until someone compared by hand.

## The answer to the question the sweep was given

*Only the two already fixed* is not it. **The same repo, the same file, holds a
third live instance of the identical shape**, and the ordering question
generalises to at least ten other sites — one of which does not need a crash to
fire."}]
